= Epistemic status =

'''Epistemic status expresses the present evidentiary standing of a claim or knowledge object.''' It communicates how a proposition is supported or contested without claiming that an assessment is final, infallible, or reducible to a universal numerical score.

A status is most informative when paired with an explanation of the supporting evidence, relevant limitations, contradictory findings, and scope of the assertion. It can change as the underlying knowledge changes.

== Standing vocabulary ==

BITwiki uses six standing terms. These terms describe broad judgments rather than thresholds automatically calculated from citation counts or prestige.

{| class="wikitable"
! Standing !! Meaning !! What to explain
|-
| '''Hypothetical''' || A conjecture, interpretation, or proposed model with limited or no direct support for the asserted proposition. || The underlying argument, assumptions, possible tests, and distinctions from established facts
|-
| '''Emerging''' || Initial support exists, but the evidence remains preliminary, narrow, fragile, or insufficiently examined. || Methods used, scope of observations, important limitations, and unresolved replication questions
|-
| '''Supported''' || Relevant evidence favors the claim, while substantial gaps, uncertainty, or competing explanations remain. || Which parts are supported, which alternatives persist, and where the inference is limited
|-
| '''Well-supported''' || Strong and appropriately independent evidence converges across relevant methods or observations. || Range of agreement, principal limitations, and important boundary conditions
|-
| '''Established''' || A robust and widely usable body of working knowledge within the relevant field and conditions, open to revision. || What is established, the evidentiary basis, and known exceptions or uncertainties
|-
| '''Disputed''' || A consequential claim, interpretation, or finding remains subject to credible unresolved conflict. || The positions, quality of supporting evidence, precise point of disagreement, and potential means of resolution
|}

'''Disputed is not a sixth rung above or below Established.''' A point of disagreement may concern an explanation or generalization even when an underlying observation is well supported. State the disagreement specifically instead of applying a broad label to an entire subject.

== Assigning a status responsibly ==

# '''Define the assertion.''' Distinguish a specific claim from the article's full subject. “A model predicts X under conditions Y” is not the same assertion as “X always occurs.”
# '''Identify the kind of support.''' Empirical results, formal proofs, archival records, engineering tests and philosophical arguments have different evidentiary roles.
# '''Evaluate quality and independence.''' Consider methods, counterexamples, possible biases, replication and whether multiple citations derive from the same observation.
# '''Examine conflicting evidence.''' An important, credible objection may require a narrower claim or a different standing.
# '''Record boundaries.''' Include relevant populations, contexts, parameter ranges, dates, versions and interpretations.
# '''Explain the judgment.''' Give readers enough reasoning to understand why the status is warranted and what could revise it.

Refer to [[BITwiki:Evidence]], [[BITwiki:Sources]] and [[BITwiki:Claim validation]] for detailed assessment methods.

== Status, confidence, and other attributes ==

Epistemic standing should not be conflated with related but different information:

{| class="wikitable"
! Attribute !! Meaning !! Examples
|-
| '''Standing''' || How a claim is presently supported or disputed || Supported, Established, Disputed
|-
| '''Uncertainty''' || What is not known precisely, remains ambiguous, or depends on assumptions || Limited sampling, unknown mechanism, disputed definition
|-
| '''Confidence rationale''' || Reasons to place a given degree of reliance on a conclusion || Agreement among independent methods, or fragility of an estimate
|-
| '''Verification history''' || Which checks have been performed, by whom or what, and with which results || Source checked, proof reviewed, test reproduced
|-
| '''Lifecycle''' || Whether a document or representation remains current or in use || Active, Superseded, Archived, Deprecated
|-
| '''Foundational role''' || Whether a statement functions as a declared starting premise or normative commitment || Axiom, principle, constitutional commitment
|}

'''Verified''' without an object and method is ambiguous: a citation may be verified as genuine while the study's conclusion remains disputed. Similarly, an archived theory may be historically important but empirically refuted.

Quantitative estimates of probability, uncertainty or calibration may be appropriate in a domain with a meaningful model. They are not mandatory or equivalent to the six standing terms.

== Applying standing to articles and claims ==

A single article may discuss established observations, emerging mechanisms, unresolved debates, and historical hypotheses. An article-level status cannot faithfully replace distinctions in the prose or the assessment of individual important claims.

Where BITwiki's knowledge-object template is used, the semantic property [[Property:Epistemic status]] is text with these six controlled values. The template's <code>status</code> parameter is explicit: leaving the field unspecified must not silently assert ''Established'' or another default. A status recorded for the page is a summary, not a machine-verifiable certificate for every sentence. More detailed disagreement or uncertainty should be explained in the article.

There is no requirement to label every assertion. A status is useful where it resolves a substantive ambiguity and can be defended by evidence.

== Revision and disagreement ==

A claim may move in either direction as stronger evidence emerges, methods improve, definitions change, or previously hidden bias becomes known. A failed replication warrants investigation into its design and comparability; one unsuccessful repetition does not mechanically invalidate a whole research program.

When a claim is disputed, distinguish areas of agreement from actual conflict. Do not manufacture symmetry between unsupported assertions and well-supported findings merely because someone contests the latter.

Review the standing when a material source is corrected or retracted, an important independent study appears, an implementation changes, or the claim is reused outside the conditions originally examined.

